WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.130
Rhiannon: There has been a fundamental
shift in the attitude of society to

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:07.840
being more aware and accepting of
those with mental health problems.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.359
Common mental disorders such as
depression and anxiety are discussed

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.660
more openly, and people are more willing
to seek help if they feel affected.

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:22.140
Today individuals who have been affected
by childhood trauma are more likely to

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.950
talk about it than in the past and society
is more aware of its potential causes.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:31.759
High profile cases of childhood abuse
have helped to highlight cases both in

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:36.289
popular culture and sporting circles
to make this all the more possible.

00:00:36.529 --> 00:00:40.950
Innovation in digital systems,
digital and social media have helped

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.480
bring mental health issues to the
fingertips of everyone supplying an

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.559
unlimited amount of information and
websites to further our knowledge and

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:50.500
understanding of mental health in general.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.190
New ideas such as mindfulness, and the
use of tried and tested techniques such as

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:01.000
yoga and meditation can help make people
aware of their own mental health and more

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.230
readily appreciate the problems of others.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.969
However, there is still social
stigma associated with mental health.

00:01:08.639 --> 00:01:12.970
Many people with mental health problems
say that social stigma and discrimination

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.990
have had a negative effect on their lives.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.010
This is because society in general
still has a stereotypical view on mental

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.840
illness and how it affects people.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.870
Some people still believe those who suffer
from mental ill health, could be violent

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.929
or dangerous when in fact they are more
likely to suffer from violence themselves.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.620
The situation is made worse when
the media portray people with

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:41.019
mental health problems as dangerous,
criminal, evil, or disabled and unable

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.990
to live normal, fulfilled lives.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.720
Most aspects of mental illness and
psychological well‐being are influenced

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:52.560
by social factors such as gender,
social class, race and ethnicity.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:53.616
A persons cultural background
can impact on attitudes to mental

00:01:53.616 --> 00:01:53.692
health on several different levels.

00:01:53.692 --> 00:01:53.813
Knowledge, attitudes and behaviours
from different social and

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:53.904
cultural groups are not the same.

00:01:53.904 --> 00:01:57.399
For some ethnic groups mental illness is
a taboo subject, and there is little open

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.330
discussion about mental health problems.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:04.500
In some communities, caring for a child or
young person with a mental health problem

00:02:04.859 --> 00:02:06.640
is seen as a family responsibility.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.179
Diagnoses are something to be kept
private and not openly discussed, even

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:16.410
with immediate family and this is partly
down to the need to preserve the family’s

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.459
reputation and status at all costs.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:22.989
Children and young people from
different ethnic or cultural groups

00:02:23.359 --> 00:02:27.060
may have a higher risk of mental health
problems because they may experience

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.470
a greater number of stressors, such
as discrimination and isolation.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.350
They may also feel split between
two communities, and therefore

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.319
feel torn between the different
social and cultural attitudes.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.470
This can be more of an issue when gender
is brought into the equation, with

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.890
girls much more likely to be affected
than boys as equality of gender is

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.650
not as prevalent in all communities.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:56.090
Shame, fear and secrecy surrounds
mental illness as the causes

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.249
are often misunderstood.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.420
In some communities, children and
young people with mental health

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.020
problems are not valued, for example,
marriage prospects can be damaged

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.690
by perceptions of mental illness.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:13.519
The culture of secrecy that surrounds
mental illness can, in part, be attributed

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.050
to misunderstandings and misconceptions
that have grown up around all aspects of

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.970
mental health problems over many years.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.570
However, on the whole the younger
generations generally have better

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.860
attitudes to mental health problems
than older age groups and will speak

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:32.850
about mental health problems more
openly among themselves in all cultures.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:38.410
So, in this vein, hopefully we continue
to see this increased positive trend

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:42.580
and work towards eradicating the
misconceptions and stigma over time.

